@* One count per label, drawn as a column — the shape both distributions on the statistics page
   are: games per difficulty, games per score band. The chrome is ChartOptions.Columns; this is
   the markup and the empty state that go with it.

   The two charts had a copy each of all of it — the guard, its sentence, the eight lines of
   series, the plot height and the row type — which was about half of each file and none of it
   anything either chart had decided for itself. They sit side by side on one tab, so the parts
   that must agree are exactly the parts that were written twice. What legitimately differs is
   the bucketing, and that is all either one does now.

   Not MonthlyGamesChart, the third column chart on the page: two series stacked into one column
   with a legend saying what the colours mean is a different picture, and it shares the chrome
   that matters through ChartOptions already. *@

@using ApexCharts

@if (Bars.Count == 0)
{
    <div class="no-data">No completed games yet.</div>
}
else
{
    <ApexChart TItem="Bar" Options="_options">
        <ApexPointSeries TItem="Bar"
                         Items="Bars"
                         Name="Games"
                         SeriesType="SeriesType.Bar"
                         XValue="@(b => b.Label)"
                         YValue="@(b => b.Count)"/>
    </ApexChart>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>One column: what it is called, and how many games are in it.</summary>
    public sealed record Bar(string Label, int Count);

    /// <summary>The columns, in the order they are drawn. None of them draws the empty note
    /// above instead — the sentence is the component's, since both charts are of the same
    /// thing.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<Bar> Bars { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The bars' colour — a <see cref="StatsTheme"/> hex, since ApexCharts cannot read a
    /// custom property.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Color { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>What the x axis is counting, where the labels alone don't say.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? XAxisTitle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The plot's height. A constant rather than a parameter, and this is the point of
    /// the component: the two charts share a row on the statistics page, so a height either one
    /// could set for itself is a height they can disagree about.</summary>
    private const int PlotHeight = 220;

    private ApexChartOptions<Bar> _options = new();

    protected override void OnParametersSet() =>
        _options = ChartOptions.Columns<Bar>(PlotHeight, [Color], XAxisTitle);
}
